WEBVTT

00:00:00.282 --> 00:00:01.389
Now we carry out the plan.

00:00:02.013 --> 00:00:03.861
Two classes follow it with the same crystals.

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:07.305
Class one's three runs end at thirty-six point

00:00:07.346 --> 00:00:09.287
zero, thirty-five point eight

00:00:09.908 --> 00:00:11.509
and thirty-six point two percent.

00:00:12.251 --> 00:00:14.575
Class two's end at thirty-four point three,

00:00:15.137 --> 00:00:16.178
thirty-four point five

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:18.063
and thirty-four point four.

00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:20.103
Both sets agree closely.

00:00:20.791 --> 00:00:23.769
But the formula says thirty-six point one percent.

00:00:24.372 --> 00:00:26.418
So which class collected accurate data,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:27.922
and how would we know?

00:00:29.457 --> 00:00:32.629
First, safety, because a plan only protects people

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:33.834
if it's actually followed.

00:00:34.578 --> 00:00:36.362
Safety glasses go on before the burner is

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:38.448
lit, and long hair is tied back.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.294
The crucible sits on its pipeclay triangle, with

00:00:41.335 --> 00:00:44.201
the lid slightly ajar, over a small blue

00:00:44.262 --> 00:00:44.602
flame.

00:00:45.324 --> 00:00:47.672
Hot apparatus is moved only with tongs, onto

00:00:47.713 --> 00:00:48.454
a heatproof mat.

00:00:48.977 --> 00:00:50.744
And the balance stays well away from the

00:00:50.785 --> 00:00:51.065
burner.

00:00:52.251 --> 00:00:54.827
Then we evaluate those practices while we work.

00:00:55.411 --> 00:00:56.896
Did each control do its job?

00:00:57.721 --> 00:00:59.506
In one run, a crystal spat out when

00:00:59.527 --> 00:01:00.790
the lid was knocked fully open.

00:01:01.152 --> 00:01:02.878
So the lid works, but only if it

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:03.581
stays in place.

00:01:04.386 --> 00:01:06.696
In another, a crucible nearly went onto the

00:01:06.757 --> 00:01:07.982
balance while still hot.

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:09.251
That's a burn risk.

00:01:10.157 --> 00:01:12.240
A hot crucible also reads low on a

00:01:12.301 --> 00:01:15.086
balance, because warm air rising around it lifts

00:01:15.147 --> 00:01:15.627
it slightly.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.154
So we add a rule: cool in the

00:01:18.195 --> 00:01:19.959
desiccator first, then weigh.

00:01:21.104 --> 00:01:23.333
And if the powder starts to darken, stop

00:01:23.375 --> 00:01:24.861
heating, because it's breaking down.

00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:28.056
That run can't measure the water, so restart

00:01:28.098 --> 00:01:29.664
gently after your teacher checks it.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:32.303
You check it, and fix it, as you

00:01:32.324 --> 00:01:32.464
go.

00:01:33.141 --> 00:01:36.368
Next, accuracy, which starts with equipment that gives

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:37.250
true readings.

00:01:37.972 --> 00:01:40.827
Before weighing, the empty balance should read zero

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:41.773
point zero zero.

00:01:42.356 --> 00:01:43.919
Then we check it with a check mass,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.705
a metal block of known mass, ten point

00:01:46.745 --> 00:01:47.647
zero zero grams.

00:01:48.349 --> 00:01:50.479
If it reads ten point zero zero, we

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.404
can trust its readings.

00:01:52.471 --> 00:01:54.571
Each sample is weighed on the same balance

00:01:54.773 --> 00:01:58.274
every time, so small differences between balances can't

00:01:58.395 --> 00:01:59.535
sneak into the subtraction.

00:02:00.318 --> 00:02:02.453
And every reading is recorded straight away.

00:02:03.562 --> 00:02:05.408
Now let's keep two words apart.

00:02:06.312 --> 00:02:08.847
Accurate means close to the accepted value.

00:02:09.591 --> 00:02:13.038
Precise means repeated readings agree closely with each

00:02:13.118 --> 00:02:14.441
other, with a small spread.

00:02:15.643 --> 00:02:18.346
From planning, valid means the method measures what

00:02:18.386 --> 00:02:19.047
it claims to,

00:02:19.688 --> 00:02:22.672
and reliable means repeating the whole experiment gives

00:02:22.732 --> 00:02:23.613
consistent results.

00:02:24.858 --> 00:02:27.549
Our accepted value is thirty-six point one percent,

00:02:27.871 --> 00:02:28.433
from the formula.

00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:30.863
Let's put both classes on the same line.

00:02:31.889 --> 00:02:34.957
Class two's results are thirty-four point three, thirty-four

00:02:34.998 --> 00:02:37.385
point five and thirty-four point four percent.

00:02:38.189 --> 00:02:38.931
Are they precise,

00:02:39.595 --> 00:02:40.679
accurate, both,

00:02:41.182 --> 00:02:41.562
or neither?

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:47.175
Precise, but not accurate.

00:02:47.719 --> 00:02:49.728
They agree closely, but they all sit well

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:51.337
below thirty-six point one.

00:02:52.142 --> 00:02:54.790
Class one is both precise and accurate.

00:02:55.674 --> 00:02:57.841
When every result is shifted the same way,

00:02:58.204 --> 00:03:00.391
something in the method is pushing them all.

00:03:01.115 --> 00:03:03.281
Class two cooled their crucibles on the open

00:03:03.322 --> 00:03:05.348
bench, so the powder took back water from

00:03:05.369 --> 00:03:06.813
the air, just like the trial run.

00:03:07.717 --> 00:03:10.343
More repeats won't fix that, because every repeat

00:03:10.404 --> 00:03:11.406
has the same problem.

00:03:12.069 --> 00:03:13.576
Only changing the method will.

00:03:14.362 --> 00:03:15.952
We'll look at these errors more closely in

00:03:16.013 --> 00:03:16.576
a later video.

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:20.785
The accepted value rests on secondary sources too.

00:03:21.469 --> 00:03:23.894
The relative atomic masses came from a published

00:03:24.035 --> 00:03:24.576
data sheet,

00:03:25.118 --> 00:03:26.661
and the hazards in our plan came from

00:03:26.702 --> 00:03:28.827
a safety data sheet, written by the chemical's

00:03:28.868 --> 00:03:29.328
supplier.

00:03:30.413 --> 00:03:32.379
How do we know a source is reliable?

00:03:33.164 --> 00:03:34.007
Ask four things.

00:03:34.693 --> 00:03:36.207
Who wrote it, and are they experts?

00:03:36.994 --> 00:03:39.127
Why was it written, to inform, or to

00:03:39.208 --> 00:03:39.469
sell?

00:03:40.355 --> 00:03:41.879
When was it written, and is it up

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:42.220
to date?

00:03:42.904 --> 00:03:45.271
And does it agree with other reliable sources?

00:03:46.095 --> 00:03:48.382
Who, why, when, and does it agree?

00:03:49.125 --> 00:03:51.589
A comment online saying copper sulfate is harmless

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.454
fails every one, and it disagrees with the

00:03:54.495 --> 00:03:55.215
safety data sheet.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.237
Then we extract only what we need.

00:03:58.782 --> 00:04:00.697
From the safety data sheet, that's three facts.

00:04:01.161 --> 00:04:03.102
Copper sulfate is harmful if swallowed,

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:06.485
it causes serious eye irritation, and it's very

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:07.726
toxic to aquatic life.

00:04:08.649 --> 00:04:10.893
Those three facts are exactly why the plan

00:04:10.974 --> 00:04:12.096
has no food or drink,

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:14.601
safety glasses, and a waste container.

00:04:15.295 --> 00:04:18.603
Finally, we acknowledge every source, in an accepted

00:04:18.784 --> 00:04:19.585
referencing style.

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.521
In the text, a short citation names the

00:04:22.683 --> 00:04:23.627
author and the year.

00:04:24.271 --> 00:04:26.394
At the end, a reference list gives the

00:04:26.475 --> 00:04:27.075
full entry:

00:04:27.597 --> 00:04:30.422
the author, the year, the title, the publisher,

00:04:30.864 --> 00:04:31.605
and where to find it.

00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:34.544
Many schools use a style called A P

00:04:34.666 --> 00:04:34.705
A.

00:04:35.429 --> 00:04:37.730
Use the style your school uses, and be

00:04:37.771 --> 00:04:40.232
consistent, so a reader can find and check

00:04:40.273 --> 00:04:41.233
the same source again.

00:04:42.077 --> 00:04:42.519
Your turn.

00:04:43.245 --> 00:04:45.934
Students made a sand and salt mixture that

00:04:45.955 --> 00:04:49.085
was exactly seventy point zero percent salt, then

00:04:49.207 --> 00:04:49.768
separated it.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:53.751
Student P recovered sixty-nine point six, sixty-nine point

00:04:53.872 --> 00:04:56.333
eight and sixty-nine point five percent salt.

00:04:56.974 --> 00:05:00.842
Student Q recovered seventy-four point one, seventy-three point

00:05:00.943 --> 00:05:02.866
nine and seventy-four point two.

00:05:03.568 --> 00:05:06.189
Compare the accuracy and precision of their results,

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.150
and give one way to make sure the

00:05:08.211 --> 00:05:09.931
balance gave accurate readings.

00:05:10.733 --> 00:05:11.546
Pause here to answer.

00:05:22.256 --> 00:05:25.108
Both sets are precise, because each student's readings

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.093
agree closely.

00:05:26.738 --> 00:05:29.095
P's are accurate, close to seventy point zero.

00:05:29.619 --> 00:05:32.120
Q's are all about four percentage points high,

00:05:32.541 --> 00:05:35.243
so something added mass, such as salt that

00:05:35.284 --> 00:05:36.083
wasn't fully dry.

00:05:36.885 --> 00:05:37.706
To check the balance,

00:05:38.349 --> 00:05:40.574
zero it, then weigh a check mass of

00:05:40.595 --> 00:05:41.236
known value.

00:05:42.019 --> 00:05:44.431
Saying Q is accurate because the readings agree

00:05:44.814 --> 00:05:45.859
is the classic slip.

00:05:46.463 --> 00:05:48.251
That's precision, not accuracy.

00:05:49.976 --> 00:05:50.398
Last one.

00:05:51.304 --> 00:05:53.650
A student needs the solubility of salt in

00:05:53.772 --> 00:05:55.716
water, and finds three sources.

00:05:56.581 --> 00:05:59.033
A chemistry data handbook from a university publisher,

00:05:59.416 --> 00:06:00.219
from twenty twenty-two.

00:06:01.145 --> 00:06:03.458
An anonymous comment under a video, with no

00:06:03.499 --> 00:06:03.739
date.

00:06:04.545 --> 00:06:07.019
And a salt supplier's safety data sheet, from

00:06:07.061 --> 00:06:07.784
twenty twenty-three.

00:06:08.648 --> 00:06:11.536
Choose the two most reliable sources and justify

00:06:11.557 --> 00:06:11.958
your choice.

00:06:12.601 --> 00:06:14.586
Then state what a reference list entry must

00:06:14.688 --> 00:06:15.068
include.

00:06:15.873 --> 00:06:16.677
Pause here to answer.

00:06:29.703 --> 00:06:30.448
A and C.

00:06:31.251 --> 00:06:33.174
Both have named, accountable authors,

00:06:33.695 --> 00:06:35.799
both are dated, and they're written to inform,

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:37.602
so they can be checked against each other.

00:06:38.425 --> 00:06:39.932
The comment has no author,

00:06:40.454 --> 00:06:42.423
no date, and no way to check it.

00:06:43.406 --> 00:06:45.654
A reference list entry needs the author, the

00:06:45.694 --> 00:06:48.443
year, the title, and the publisher or where

00:06:48.483 --> 00:06:48.885
to find it.

00:06:49.749 --> 00:06:51.697
Choosing the comment because lots of people liked

00:06:51.739 --> 00:06:53.205
it is the classic slip.

00:06:53.789 --> 00:06:55.924
Popular isn't the same as reliable.

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.080
So we follow the plan safely, and check

00:07:00.141 --> 00:07:01.145
our safety as we go.

00:07:01.829 --> 00:07:03.552
We set up the balance to read truly,

00:07:03.894 --> 00:07:06.399
and judge accuracy against an accepted value.

00:07:07.262 --> 00:07:08.966
And we choose reliable sources,

00:07:09.508 --> 00:07:11.533
take what we need, and acknowledge them.

00:07:12.436 --> 00:07:14.440
One of those sources, the data sheet, is

00:07:14.502 --> 00:07:15.884
really a periodic table.

00:07:16.487 --> 00:07:18.074
Let's go back inside its atoms.

00:07:18.718 --> 00:07:20.989
How does s p d f notation locate

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.276
the electrons in an atom?

00:07:24.961 --> 00:07:25.784
Here are the key notes.

00:07:26.307 --> 00:07:27.855
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:27.895 --> 00:07:28.317
to copy them.
